Z||000004||Old Lar||10-18-2001||08:29 AM||||If you want a restoration project, get it for a couple of hundred bucks and plan on spending several thousand to fix it.  Or spend the several thousand and find a good one initially.  Either way you'll be spending a few grand.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||10-18-2001||10:11 AM||||Exactly, I love working on cars so I bought a Fiero that needed a little TLC.  If you don't want any hassles, save up and get one that has fewer problems.<P>All up to you and it depends on how you look at it.  Building cars is like a hobby for me, it can be a lot of fun and rewarding in the end.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue4.gif">    <BR>T-T-F-N, Ta Ta For Now!<BR><A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/tatooine/spielberg/241/" TARGET=_blank>Tigger's Fiero Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||10-18-2001||02:49 PM||||I'm with Tigger on this one.  Working on Fieros has become a hobby for me.  I now go look for the cheapest Fiero I can find and see how little money I can spend fixing it up.  My $750 86SE turned out to be a great car.  My sons $250 85SE, however, needs to be rebuilt from the ground up, but I have really found some great deals on the parts for it.  All depends on what you like doing.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dragon||10-18-2001||08:41 PM||||There might be a couple down here in Brevard to check out.  One has slid into a bridge ant sure how much damage and the other has a blown motor<P>Plus I got a call on 86 GT the other day<P>Dragon<BR>    Space Coast Fieros<BR>        Florida Fiero Council<BR>        <A HREF="http://floridafieros.org/daytona2002/" TARGET=_blank>http://floridafieros.org/daytona2002/</A> ||||reg||1
